      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "The morning most of us have",
        "body": "<p>The alarm ends. Before the day has named itself, the phone is already in your hand — a message from last night, a headline, a notification you don't remember enabling.</p><p>By the time you are fully awake, the day is already running someone else's agenda. It started two minutes ago, and it is already not yours.</p>",
        "contrast_a_label": "Most mornings",
        "contrast_a": "<p>Alarm → phone → messages → reaction</p>",
        "contrast_b_label": "A different beginning",
        "contrast_b": "<p>Alarm → light → a small ritual → orientation → the day, entered on purpose</p>"
      }
    },
    "reasoning": {
      "type": "mirellis-pdp-reasoning",
      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "How this came to exist",
        "intro": "<p>Nothing here began as a product. It began with an observation.</p>",
        "finding_label": "Finding 002",
        "finding_line": "The first thirty minutes after waking are not like the rest of the day.",
        "body": "<p>The nervous system, in that window, appears unusually receptive — it takes on whatever signal arrives first. Modern life fills that window with a screen.</p><p>So we asked one question: given that, what would most help the body cross from sleep into wakefulness — before screens, before caffeine, before language?</p><p>That question opens a landscape of possibilities — light, hydration, temperature, breath, scent, sound, and slow oral-sensory activity. None is obviously correct on its own. We were drawn to a slow, repetitive, oral-sensory ritual performed before the first screen: it occupies attention gently, sits before language and notification, and is easy to repeat at the same moment each day.</p><p>Only then did we ask what form it should take. The answer was an Ayurvedic practice older than any of this research.</p>",
        "steps": "Transition, Observation, Finding, Question, Intervention, Ritual, Product",
        "link1_label": "Read Finding 002 →",
        "link1_url": "https://mirellis.in/blogs/journal/the-first-30-minutes-after-waking-are-not-like-the-rest-of-the-day",
        "link2_label": "The full reasoning →",
        "link2_url": "https://mirellis.in/pages/intervention-morning-clarity"
      }
    },
    "lineage": {
      "type": "mirellis-pdp-lineage",
      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "The practice we chose",
        "heading": "Kavala — the classical morning practice, honestly bounded.",
        "tradition_tag": "Classical Ayurvedic tradition",
        "tradition": "<p>Oil pulling — <em>Kavala</em> and <em>Gandusha</em> — is described in the classical Ayurvedic texts as a morning oral practice, part of <em>dinacharya</em>, the daily routine. Tradition associates it with oral cleanliness, freshness, and a clear beginning to the day.</p>",
        "boundary": "<p>That is what tradition describes. It is not the same as modern clinical proof, and we will not pretend it is. We keep tradition and evidence separate on purpose — and where we cannot yet point to strong evidence, we would rather say nothing than overstate. That restraint is the honest version of an Ayurvedic ritual, and we think it is the only one worth making.</p>",
        "expand_label": "More on Kavala vs. Gandusha and their place in dinacharya →",
        "expand_url": "/pages/the-first-signals-of-the-morning"
      }
